   "Pi header pins use physical numbering: GPIO4 = 7, GPIO14 = 8, GPIO15 = 10, GND = 9, as the image's pin labels show.",
   "Breadboard naming follows the catalogue: bank 'b' holds rows f-j (where the buttons sit in the image) and bank 't' holds rows a-e (LED, resistor). The blue ground rail next to rows a-e is named 'tn'. Rail holes are numbered 1-25 left to right in the image: left button wire at tn.1, resistor at tn.10, Pi GND wire at tn.19, right button wire at tn.25. A rail is one strip, so the exact index does not matter electrically.",
   "The image shows the LED legs at row e (the text says D). Image used: long leg (anode) in column 16, short leg (cathode) in column 15. The resistor goes from 15t.a (the LED cathode strip) to the GND rail, and the GPIO4 wire goes to 16t.a (the anode strip).",
   "Each button sits in columns 1 and 3 (left) or 28 and 30 (right), with legs in rows h and j. Side 1 (columns 1 and 30) goes through a black wire to the GND rail. Side 2 (columns 3 and 28) goes to GPIO14 (left) or GPIO15 (right) through an orange wire. The text puts the legs in rows H and J, so the buttons do not straddle the centre gap and the catalogue's across-the-gap fit is not used.",
   "For the pin-to-socket wires (jumper-wire-mf), end 'a' is the female socket end on the Pi header and end 'b' is the male pin end in the breadboard. The two pin-to-pin wires are the black button-to-rail wires.",
   "Each physical connection has both a joined and an electrical edge."
